ets the vertical line \\(x=t+j+1\\) at  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{j}=(t+j+1,\\;j-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(S_j\\) contains **all** lattice points whose abscissa is either \\(t+j\\) or \\(t+j+1\\) and whose ordinate does not exceed \\(j\\); i.e. it contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{(t+j,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le j\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{(t+j+1,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le j-1\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(j\\le k\\) and \\(t=n-k\\), the condition \\(b\\le j\\) guarantees that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>t\\) satisfies \\(b\\le k\\) and therefore belongs to the set (2) for the unique index \\(j=b\\). Hence **every** lattice point whose abscissa exceeds \\(t\\) lies on exactly one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   – **Step 3.** The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all required lattice points.\n\n   The construction works for every admissible \\(k\\); when \\(k=n\\) (resp. \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\)) the bound shows that such a configuration cannot exist, so the list above is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n*Horizontal* lines (\\(y=c\\)) intersect \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the points with ordinate \\(c\\) and abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n+1-c\\)).  \n*Vertical* lines (\\(x=c\\)) have the same bound.  \n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\)* (\\(x+y=c\\)) contain at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.\n\nHence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).**  \n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Write its reduced rational slope as \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\((p,q)=1\\). Two distinct lattice points on such a line differ by a vector \\((q, p)\\); therefore the abscissae of points on the line differ by multiples of \\(q\\). Inside the interval \\([1,n]\\) there can be at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lattice points. Since \\(q\\ge2\\) for a sunny line, the maximal number of points on a sunny line is  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. Using (3)–(4),\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m-k)2m+km\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow 2m-k-1\\ge0,\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1-k)(2m+1)+km\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration can contain at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even and at most \\(n-2\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of configurations for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that the bounds (6)–(7) are sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny. For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(L_c\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. General construction for admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6) (if \\(n\\) even) or (7) (if \\(n\\) odd) and put  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall keep}).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (6)–(7) we have \\(t\\ge1\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non‑sunny, and together they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y=-2x+(2t+2j).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(S_j\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=t+j\\) at  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(t+j,\\;j),\n\\]\n\nand meets the vertical line \\(x=t+j+1\\) at  \n\n\\[\nQ_j=(t+j+1,\\;j-1).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(S_j\\) contains exactly the lattice points listed in (2).  \n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(a>t\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(t=n-k\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(t+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); set \\(j=b\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge t+j\\) and \\(a\\le t+j+1\\) (otherwise \\(a\\) would be larger than \\(t+k+1=n+1\\)), we have either \\(a=t+j\\) or \\(a=t+j+1\\). In the first case \\((a,b)=(t+j,j)=P_j\\); in the second case \\((a,b)=(t+j+1,j-1)=Q_j\\). In both situations \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).\n\nConsequently **every** point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with abscissa larger than \\(t\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), while the remaining points are covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Maximal values of \\(k\\)\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, the bound (6) gives \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nOur construction works for every \\(k\\le n-1\\); for \\(k=n\\) it would require \\(t=0\\) and the sunny lines (9) would have to cover the points with abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n\\). Because each sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor<n\\) points, \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, so \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, the bound (7) gives \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nOur construction works for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nIf \\(k=n-1\\) (i.e. \\(t=1\\)), the single non‑sunny line would be \\(x=1\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to cover the points with abscissa \\(2,\\dots ,n\\). The total number of points to be covered is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n)=\\frac{(n-1)(n+2)}2\\), which exceeds \\((n-1)\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\); therefore \\(k=n-1\\) cannot be realised. Hence the maximal admissible value is \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly the integers described in the statement of the theorem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
