\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the right–hand side of (1) is in fact \\(n-1\\); the case\n   \\(k=n\\) can occur only when \\(n=3\\) (see the construction below).\n\n2. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Write  \n\n   \\[\n   t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n   m=t\\;(n\\text{ even}),\\; m=t+1\\;(n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For every integer \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\le t-1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_d\\) is sunny and contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(S\\).\n   The family \\(\\{L_d\\mid |d|\\le t-1\\}\\) consists of \\(2t-1=n-1\\) distinct\n   sunny lines.\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(S\\) that are **not** on any of the lines (2) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,b)\\mid b=t+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   They all lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny and\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Realising any \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \n   Choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\) with\n   \\(|d_i|\\le t-1\\) and take the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{d_i}\\).\n   The set of points not covered by these \\(k\\) lines is a subset of the\n   set (3); consequently it is contained in at most \\(n-k\\) rows of the\n   triangle.  Cover each of those rows by a horizontal line\n   \\(y=b\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\).  The obtained family consists of exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny horizontal lines, and it\n   covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(S\\) consists of the three points\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  Taking three distinct lines, each passing\n   through a single point and having a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\),\n   gives a configuration with \\(k=3=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\); the construction above shows that\n   every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n   For \\(n=3\\) the extra value \\(k=3\\) is also attainable, and the bound\n   (1) is sharp.\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one\nstated in the verdict.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge4:\\;k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1;\\qquad\n       \\text{for }n=3:\\;k=0,1,2,3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
