            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   N_n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Any sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\); moreover, when \\(n\\) is odd only the line \\(y=x\\) attains the bound \\(M_n\\), every other sunny line contains at most \\(M_n-1\\) points.\n\n   *Proof idea.*  \n   Write a sunny line in primitive integer form \\(p x+q y=r\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Moving from one lattice point of the line to the next changes the sum\n   \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Because all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), the number of\n   admissible values of \\(a+b\\) on the line is at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   When \\(|p+q|=1\\) the line has slope \\(1\\) (i.e. \\(y=x+d\\)); such a line\n   indeed contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points,\n   which is at most \\(M_n\\), and equality occurs only for \\(d=0\\) when\n   \\(n\\) is odd. ∎\n\n3. **Counting incidences.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   Using Lemma 1, the total number of incidences satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   N_n\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,M_n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N_n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   When \\(n\\) is odd we can sharpen (2) using the second part of Lemma 1:\n   at most one sunny line can contain \\(M_n\\) points, all the others contain\n   at most \\(M_n-1\\) points.  Re‑applying (1) with this refined estimate\n   yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge5\\text{ odd}).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the bound (2) is sharp (see the construction below).\n\n4. **Realising the bounds.**  \n   *Base covering.*  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\); thus \\(k=0\\) is always\n   attainable.\n\n   *Interior triangle.*  \n   Remove the first row and the first column of the lattice triangle:\n   set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) the set \\(T_n\\) becomes\n   the triangle \\(S_{n-2}\\).  Consequently \\(|T_n|=N_{n-2}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   s_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   consider the family  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x+d,\\qquad d=-s_n,\\dots ,s_n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Every line in (5) is sunny.  When \\(n\\) is even each of them contains\n   exactly \\(s_n+1=\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\); when \\(n\\) is odd the\n   central line \\(d=0\\) contains \\(s_n+2=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points and the\n   remaining \\(2s_n\\) lines contain \\(s_n+1=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) points each.\n   In either case the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the\n   whole family (5) is exactly \\(|T_n|\\); hence the family (5) together\n   with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying the upper bounds (2)–(3).  \n   Choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct lines from the family (5); denote this\n   sub‑family by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  The points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by\n   \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) lie in at most \\((n-2)-k\\) rows (or columns) of the\n   triangle \\(T_n\\).  Each such row can be covered by a single horizontal\n   line \\(y=b\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=a\\)), which is non‑sunny.  Adding\n   those horizontal (or vertical) lines to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) together with the\n   two fixed lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines\n   containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Because the number of extra non‑sunny rows needed is \\((n-2)-k\\), the\n   total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{the fixed }x=1,y=1)+k\\;(\\text{chosen sunny})+(n-2-k)\n   =n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) allowed by (2)–(3) is realizable.\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) can be covered by three\n   pairwise‑different sunny lines, e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k=3\\) is also attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument gives the universal upper bounds\n   \\(k\\le n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) (with the\n   exceptional case \\(n=3\\) where \\(k\\le3\\)).  The explicit constructions\n   above show that every integer up to those bounds can indeed be achieved.\n   Therefore the list of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one\n   stated in the verdict.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\\n&n\\ge4\\text{ even}:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\\n&n\\ge5\\text{ odd}:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
