e point \\((n,1)\\).  \nAdd any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\ny=2x-(2n-1)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\) and meets \\((n,1)\\).  \nNow we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe required set of points is  \n\n\\[\nP_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFour families of three lines, one for each possible value of \\(k\\), are listed below.\n\n| \\(k\\) | three lines (all distinct) | sunny? |\n|------|----------------------------|--------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;y=2x-3\\) | only \\(y=2x-3\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=2x-3,\\;y=3x-5\\) | the last two |\n| 3 | \\(y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2,\\;y=4x-3\\) | all three |\n\nEach family consists of three distinct lines, covers the three points, and has the prescribed number of sunny lines. Hence for \\(n=3\\) all values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary\n\nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset P_{n}.\n\\]\n\n* A vertical line can contain all points of \\(C\\).  \n* Any non‑vertical line meets \\(C\\) in **at most one** point, because two distinct points of \\(C\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n* In particular, a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) also meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.\n\nIf the configuration contained **no** vertical line, then each point of \\(C\\) would have to be covered by a different line, forcing at least \\(n\\) distinct lines. Since we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, the vertical line \\(x=1\\) must belong to the configuration. Hence **one** non‑sunny line is forced.\n\nApply the same argument to the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf the line \\(y=1\\) were absent, each point of \\(R\\) would need a distinct line, again requiring at least \\(n\\) lines. Consequently the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) must also be present, unless we already used a diagonal line to cover the whole row. Because the diagonal family consists of lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\), none of them contains two points of \\(R\\); therefore \\(y=1\\) is forced as a second non‑sunny line.\n\nThus **any** admissible configuration contains at least two non‑sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Excluding \\(k\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge 4\\) and that a configuration contains at least two sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \nBy (2) the configuration then has **exactly** two non‑sunny lines.  \nFrom the discussion in §2 we may assume that these two lines are the forced vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the forced horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (any other choice is symmetric).\n\nRemove the points already covered by \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThe remaining points are those with coordinates \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); they form a smaller triangular set\n\n\\[\nP'_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n,\\;2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe column \\(a=2\\) of \\(P'_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) points:\n\\[\n(2,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(2,n).\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can intersect this column in at most one point, and the only remaining non‑sunny line (namely \\(y=1\\)) does **not** intersect the column at all. Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines would be required to cover column 2, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines are available.\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) we must have \\(k\\le 1\\).  \nTogether with the constructions of §1.1 and §1.2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 4\\text{ the only possible values are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\nCombining the constructions for all admissible values of \\(k\\) with the impossibility results proved above, we obtain the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3 :\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\\n&n\\ge 4 :\\quad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nAll listed values are realised by explicit families of lines, and no other value can occur. ∎"
