e lines, say \\(\\ell\\), contains **at least two**\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Choose two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in\\ell\\) such that\n   \\(P\\) does not lie on any other line of the family (such a point exists,\n   otherwise every point of \\(\\ell\\) would be double‑covered and we could delete\n   \\(\\ell\\) altogether, contradicting the minimality of the family).  \n\n   Let \\(R\\) be any point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on \\(\\ell\\) (there is one because\n   \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical and the set \\(T_{n}\\) is not collinear).  Consider the\n   line \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) through the pair \\(\\{P,R\\}\\).  Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y_{R}-y_{P}}{x_{R}-x_{P}} .\n   \\]\n   By a direct check the three forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) cannot occur:\n   * \\(m=0\\) would force \\(y_{R}=y_{P}\\) and then \\(R\\) would lie on the horizontal\n     line through \\(P\\), which is not a line of the original family (otherwise\n     \\(P\\) would be double‑covered).  \n   * \\(m=\\infty\\) would give \\(x_{R}=x_{P}\\); this would make \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) vertical,\n     but then \\(R\\) would belong to the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\) already present in\n     the family, again contradicting the choice of \\(R\\).  \n   * \\(m=-1\\) would imply \\(x_{R}+y_{R}=x_{P}+y_{P}\\).  Since \\(P\\) lies on the line\n     \\(\\ell\\) that already contains at least two points of \\(T_{n}\\), the equality\n     forces \\(R\\) to be on the same line of slope \\(-1\\) as \\(\\ell\\); such a line is\n     non‑sunny and would already be present, contradicting the choice of \\(R\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) is sunny.  Replace the original line \\(\\ell\\) by the two\n   lines \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) and the line \\(\\ell^{\\prime\\prime}\\) through the pair\n   \\(\\{Q,R\\}\\).  The new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\) (every point that was on \\(\\ell\\) is now on either\n   \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) or \\(\\ell^{\\pr