\ell\\cup A\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nFor this choice the line \\(\\ell_{1}=PR\\) is **sunny** (its slope is none of\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Define \\(\\ell_{2}\\) to be the line through the pair\n\\(\\{Q,R\\}\\).  The three lines \\(\\ell,\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) are distinct, and the\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) that was on \\(\\ell\\) is now on either \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (the\npoint \\(P\\)) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (the point \\(Q\\)); all other points are unchanged,\nso \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines has increased by **exactly one**: \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is sunny,\nwhereas \\(\\ell_{2}\\) has the same slope as \\(\\ell\\) (because both contain the\npoint \\(Q\\) and the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is preserved when we replace the second\npoint of \\(\\ell\\) by \\(R\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Obtaining all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the covering \\(\\mathcal V_{n}\\) of §1.1, which has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\nApply the Lemma repeatedly:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{n}\\; \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lemma}}\\;\n\\mathcal F_{1}\\; \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lemma}}\\;\n\\mathcal F_{2}\\; \\dots\\; \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lemma}}\\;\n\\mathcal F_{n},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\nBecause the Lemma works for every \\(t<n\\), we obtain a covering for each\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) produced by the last step coincides with the\nall‑sunny construction of §1.2, confirming that the process indeed reaches the\nextreme case \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there\nexists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
