e $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in \\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nand among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Two elementary coverings**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non‑sunny) cover \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\)* – a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines each joining a point of the hypotenuse\n   \\[\n   H_{n}= \\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   with the point \\((1,2)\\).  \n   The line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   m_{i}= \\frac{n- i}{\\,n+2-2i\\,},\n   \\qquad 1\\le i\\le n .\n   \\]\n   One checks that \\(m_{i}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all these lines are sunny.\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the line joining \\((1,2)\\) with the unique\n   hypotenuse point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for which the three points are collinear; a short\n   algebraic verification shows that this \\(i\\) is precisely \\(i=n+2-a-b\\).  Consequently the\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n2. **Increasing the number of sunny lines one by one**  \n   *Lemma (Sunny‑increase).*  \n   Suppose we have a covering of \\(T_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k<n\\)).  Then we can modify the family to obtain a new\n   covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Proof of the Lemma.*  \n   Because the covering uses only \\(n\\) lines while \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2>n\\) for\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\), at least one of the lines, say \\(\\ell\\), contains **at least two**\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Choose two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in\\ell\\) such that\n   \\(P\\) does not lie on any other line of the family (such a point exists,\n   otherwise every point of \\(\\ell\\) would be double‑covered and we could delete\n   \\(\\ell\\) altogether, contradicting the minimality of the family).  \n\n   Let \\(R\\) be any point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on \\(\\ell\\) (there is one because\n   \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical and the set \\(T_{n}\\) is not collinear).  Consider the\n   line \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) through the pair \\(\\{P,R\\}\\).  Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y_{R}-y_{P}}{x_{R}-x_{P}} .\n   \\]\n   By a direct check the three forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) cannot occur:\n   * \\(m=0\\) would force \\(y_{R}=y_{P}\\) and then \\(R\\) would lie on the horizontal\n     line through \\(P\\), which is not a line of the original family (otherwise\n     \\(P\\) would be double‑covered).  \n   * \\(m=\\infty\\) would give \\(x_{R}=x_{P}\\); this would make \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) vertical,\n     but then \\(R\\) would belong to the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\) already present in\n     the family, again contradicting the choice of \\(R\\).  \n   * \\(m=-1\\) would imply \\(x_{R}+y_{R}=x_{P}+y_{P}\\).  Since \\(P\\) lies on the line\n     \\(\\ell\\) that already contains at least two points of \\(T_{n}\\), the equality\n     forces \\(R\\) to be on the same line of slope \\(-1\\) as \\(\\ell\\); such a line is\n     non‑sunny and would already be present, contradicting the choice of \\(R\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) is sunny.  Replace the o