ime\\prime}\\); all other points are unchanged), and\n   the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one (the line \\(\\ell^{\\prime}\\) is\n   sunny, while \\(\\ell^{\\prime\\prime}\\) has the same slope as \\(\\ell\\) and therefore\n   is non‑sunny).  ∎\n\n3. **Conclusion**  \n   Starting from the covering with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (the vertical family) and\n   applying the Lemma repeatedly we obtain coverings with \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) sunny\n   lines.  Together with the explicit all‑sunny construction for \\(k=n\\) we have\n   exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in \\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two extreme coverings\n\n#### 1.1  No sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{n}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\)\nsatisfies \\(x=a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\) of the family.\nThus a covering with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nConsider the point  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,2)\\in\\mathbb R^{2}\\setminus T_{n}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\in H_{n},\n\\qquad\nL_{i}= \\text{the line through }P\\text{ and }H_{i}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{n+1-i-2}{\\,i-1\\,}= \\frac{n-i}{\\,n+2-2i\\,}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n* \\(m_{i}\\neq 0\\) (the numerator \\(n-i\\) is non‑zero unless \\(i=n\\), in which case\n  the denominator equals \\(-n\\neq 0\\));\n* \\(m_{i}\\neq \\infty\\) (the denominator \\(n+2-2i\\) never vanishes for integer\n  \\(i\\) in the given range);\n* \\(m_{i}\\neq -1\\) because solving \\(\\frac{n-i}{n+2-2i}=-1\\) gives \\(n+2= i\\), which\n  is impossible for \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\nHence every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni=n+2-a-b\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that the three points \\((a,b),\\;P,\\;H_{i}\\) are collinear,\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{i}\\).  Consequently the family\n\\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_{n}\\) and consists solely of sunny\nlines.  Thus a covering with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A tool for increasing the number of sunny lines\n\nWe now prove the Lemma announced in the sketch.\n\n> **Lemma (Sunny‑increase).**  \n> Let \\(0\\le k<n\\).  Suppose there is a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n> covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists a\n> family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing\n> exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains\nat least two points of \\(T_{n}\\); denote such a line by \\(\\ell\\) and pick two distinct\npoints \\(P,Q\\in\\ell\\cap T_{n}\\).\n\nIf every point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_{n}\\) also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal F\\), then\nremoving \\(\\ell\\) would leave a covering by at most \\(n-1\\) lines, contradicting the\nminimality of the size \\(n\\).  Hence we may choose \\(P\\) so that it is **unique** to\n\\(\\ell\\) (i.e. \\(P\\) does not belong to any other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\)).\n\nSelect a point \\(R\\in T_{n}\\setminus\\ell\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\ell\\) is\nnot the whole set).  Consider the line \\(\\ell_{1}\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(R\\).  Its slope\nis\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_{R}-y_{P}}{x_{R}-x_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(m=0\\) then \\(y_{R}=y_{P}\\); the horizontal line through \\(P\\) would contain\n  \\(R\\) and therefore would already belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\), contradicting the choice\n  of \\(R\\).\n\n* If \\(m=\\infty\\) then \\(x_{R}=x_{P}\\); the vertical line through \\(P\\) would contain\n  \\(R\\) and again would be a member of \\(\\mathcal F\\), a contradiction.\n\n* If \\(m=-1\\) then \\(x_{R}+y_{R}=x_{P}+y_{P}\\).  Since \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) and\n  \\(\\ell\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_{n}\\), the equality forces \\(R\\) to lie\n  on the same line of slope \\(-1\\) as \\(\\ell\\); such a line is non‑sunny and already\n  present in \\(\\mathcal F\\), contradicting the choice of \\(R\\).\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is sunny.  Let \\(\\ell_{2}\\) be the line through the remaining pair\n\\(\\{Q,R\\}\\).  The three lines \\(\\ell,\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) are distinct, and the set\n\\((\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}\\) still consists of exactly\n\\(n\\) lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) that was on \\(\\ell\\) is now on either \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (the point\n\\(P\\)) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (the point \\(Q\\)); all other points are untouched.  Hence the\nnew family still covers \\(T_{n}\\).  The number of sunny lines has increased by one,\nbecause \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is sunny while \\(\\ell_{2}\\) has the same slope as \\(\\ell\\) and\ntherefore is non‑sunny.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe start from the covering with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (the vertical family\n\\(\\mathcal V_{n}\\)).  Applying the Lemma repeatedly we obtain, for each integer\n\\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\), a covering \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) of \\(T_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) distinct\nlines that contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  In particular \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) is a\ncovering with all lines sunny; the explicit construction of Section 1.2 shows that\n\\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) can be chosen as the family \\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) described there.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, **every** non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised as the\nnumber of sunny lines in a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  ∎"
