thcal F' =\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nEvery point that was on \\(\\ell\\) is now on either \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (the point\n\\(P\\)) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (the point \\(Q\\)); all other points of \\(T_{n}\\)\nare unchanged, so \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines has increased by one, because \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is\nsunny while \\(\\ell_{2}\\) has the same (non‑sunny) slope as \\(\\ell\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe start from the covering \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) of Section 2, which has\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  \nApplying the Lemma repeatedly we obtain, for each integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na covering \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) of \\(T_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncontains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\nIn particular, after \\(n\\) applications we obtain a covering with\n\\(k=n\\) sunny lines; this shows that the Lemma can be applied as long as\n\\(k<n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny\nlines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines in the plane that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and among those lines exactly \\(k\\) are\nsunny.  ∎"
