, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict.**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n**b. Method sketch.**  \nThe set of required points is the integer lattice triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n1. **A lemma.**  \nFor any integer \\(m\\ge1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct *sunny* lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\).  \nThe construction uses a point \\(P\\) with non‑integer coordinates strictly inside \\(T_m\\) and the \\(m\\) lines joining \\(P\\) with the \\(m\\) points of the hypotenuse \\(\\{(i,m+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is not on a vertical or horizontal line and does not satisfy \\(x+y=m+1\\), none of these lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_m\\) lies on the line joining \\(P\\) with the unique point of the hypotenuse that lies on the ray from \\(P\\) through the given point, so the \\(m\\) lines cover \\(T_m\\).\n\n2. **Construction for a general \\(k\\).**  \nLet \\(k\\) be given and put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   * Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t\n\\]\n\n(which are non‑sunny) to cover the leftmost \\(t\\) columns of the triangle.  \n   * After removing these columns the remaining points form a triangle congruent to \\(T_k\\) (shift the coordinates by \\((t,0)\\)).  \n   * Apply the lemma to this triangle to obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering it.  \n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny and \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Edge cases.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   * \\(k=n\\): take \\(t=0\\) and apply the lemma directly to the whole triangle \\(T_n\\).  \n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are precisely the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose real numbers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n0<\\alpha<1,\\qquad 0<\\beta<1,\\qquad \\alpha+\\beta<1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nP=(1+\\alpha,\\;1+\\beta).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n1<1+\\alpha< m,\\qquad 1<1+\\beta< m,\\qquad (1+\\alpha)+(1+\\beta)=2+\\alpha+\\beta< m+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(P\\) lies strictly inside the triangle \\(T_m\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nB_i=(i,\\;m+1-i)\n\\]\n\n(the \\(m\\) lattice points on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=m+1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=\\overline{PB_i}\\qquad (1\\le i\\le m).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(m+1-i)-(1+\\beta)}{\\,i-(1+\\alpha)}=\n\\frac{m-i-\\beta}{\\,i-1-\\alpha}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are non‑integers, the denominator in (2) never vanishes; thus \\(L_i\\) is not vertical.  \nThe numerator in (2) is never zero because \\(m-i-\\beta\\neq0\\) (again \\(\\beta\\notin\\mathbb Z\\)); hence \\(L_i\\) is not horizontal.  \nFinally, \\(L_i\\) cannot have slope \\(-1\\): a slope \\(-1\\) would give  \n\n\\[\nm-i-\\beta=-(i-1-\\alpha)\\Longrightarrow \\alpha+\\beta=m-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts \\(\\alpha+\\beta<1\\le m-1\\).  Consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are distinct because they have distinct second endpoints \\(B_i\\).\n\nNow let \\(Q=(a,b)\\in T_m\\).  The ray starting at \\(P\\) and passing through \\(Q\\) meets the hypotenuse \\(a+b=m+1\\) at a unique point \\(B\\) of the form \\((i,m+1-i)\\) with integer \\(i\\).  By construction \\(B\\) is one of the points \\(B_i\\), and the line \\(PQ\\) coincides with \\(L_i\\).  Hence \\(Q\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Therefore the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i\\) contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n).\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \nIf \\(t\\ge1\\) consider the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line in (3) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le t\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) of (3); thus all points of the leftmost \\(t\\) columns of the triangle are covered.\n\n**Step 2 – the remaining triangle.**  \nDelete the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=t\\).  The uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the change of variables  \n\n\\[\na'=a-t,\\qquad b'=b .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b'=(a-t)+b\\le n+1-t=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set is exactly the triangle \\(T_k\\) (translated by \\((t,0)\\))