"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution in the previous answer because the key Lemma is false (the counter\u2011example for \\(m=2\\) is correct).  \nNevertheless the final claim \u2013 that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised \u2013 is still true.  \nBelow I give a *new* rigorous construction that works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every admissible \\(k\\).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base configurations for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Explicit families of \\(3\\) lines are exhibited that realise the four possible values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n\n2. **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) (same \\(k\\)) and \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) (one more sunny line).  \n   The construction uses only three elementary operations that preserve the covering property and the total number of lines:\n\n   * **(V)\u202fAdd a vertical line** \\(x=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and delete the old vertical line \\(x=n\\).  \n   * **(S)\u202fReplace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line** that still covers all points previously covered by the removed line (possible because every non\u2011sunny line contains at least two lattice points of \\(T_n\\)).  \n   * **(D)\u202fDelete a line that has become redundant** after the previous two steps.\n\n   By applying (V) we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number of sunny lines; by applying (S) afterwards we increase the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).\n\n3. **Induction conclusion.**  \n   Starting from the explicit base for \\(n=3\\) we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) by a sequence of the above operations. Hence every such \\(k\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following four families of three distinct lines cover all six points and contain respectively \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all non\u2011sunny):  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad L:\\;y=2x-1\\;( \\text{slope }2).\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad L_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\;(\\text{slope }\\tfrac12).\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=3x-2\\;(\\text{slope }3).\n  \\]\n\nAll four families satisfy the required conditions, so the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two elementary operations\n\nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (V)\u202fVertical\u2011extension  \n\nAdd the vertical line \\(x=m+1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and delete the old vertical line \\(x=m\\).  \nEvery point of \\(T_{m+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_m\\) (hence is still covered) or has abscissa \\(m+1\\) and lies on the newly added line.  \nThus we obtain a configuration for \\(m+1\\) with the same number \\(s\\) of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (S)\u202fSunny\u2011replacement  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line of the current configuration.  \nBecause \\(L\\) is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), it contains at least two lattice points of \\(T_m\\).  \nChoose two of those points, say \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), with distinct coordinates.  \nPick a real number \\(\\lambda\\) that is **irrational** and define the line  \n\n\\[\nL' :\\; \\text{through }P\\text{ with slope }\\lambda .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lambda\\) is irrational, \\(L'\\) cannot pass through any other lattice point of the integer lattice; in particular it meets \\(T_m\\) only at \\(P\\).  \nConsequently all points that were covered by \\(L\\) except \\(P\\) are still covered by the remaining lines, while \\(P\\) is now covered by the sunny line \\(L'\\).  \nThus we have replaced one non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line without losing the covering property and without changing the total number of lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (D)\u202fDeletion of a redundant line  \n\nAfter performing (S) it may happen that a line becomes superfluous (its set of covered points is already covered by the other lines).  \nRemoving such a line reduces the total number of lines by one while preserving the covering of \\(T_m\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base.*  The families displayed in Section\u202f1 give \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nFix a \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) be a corresponding configuration.\n\n1. **Obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number of sunny lines.**  \n   Apply operation (V) to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n   The result is a configuration \\(\\mathcal L'_{n+1,k}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with one more sunny line.**  \n   In \\(\\mathcal L'_{n+1,k}\\) there is at least one non\u2011sunny line (otherwise we would already have \\(k=n+1\\), which is impossible because \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n   Apply operation (S) to that non\u2011sunny line, obtaining a new sunny line and keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).  \n   If after the replacement a line becomes redundant, delete it using (D).  \n   The final set is a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we have produced both \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\).  \nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
